Level 1 — Normal object creation

Problem:

I have a class and I need an object.

A obj = new A();
Level 2 — Inheritance

Problem:

I have a common parent and want specialized child classes.

class B extends A
Level 3 — Runtime Polymorphism

Problem:

I want one parent reference to work with different child objects.

A obj = new B();

And when show() is overridden:

obj.show();

Java uses dynamic method dispatch to select the implementation at runtime.

Level 4 — Inner Class

Different problem:

I have a class that is strongly related to another class, so I want to define it inside the outer class.

class A {

    class B {
    }
}
Level 5 — Anonymous Inner Class

Another different problem:

I need a small class implementation only once, and I don't want to give that class a name.

A obj = new A() {

    void show() {
    }
};



